---
import Product from '../../layouts/Product.astro';
import PersonHouseholdRecords from '../../components/PersonHouseholdRecords.astro';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const product = products.find((p) => p.key === 'base-registry')!;
---
<Product
  product={product}
  heading="Base Registry Engine keeps the records your public services rely on."
  description="Use Base Registry Engine to maintain the records you are responsible for, such as records about people, businesses, licences, places or assets. It applies your access rules and keeps a history of changes. Your approved staff applications and online services can work with the register through it."
>
  <section id="product-example" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="registry-lifecycle-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Example: a fictional population register</p>
        <h2 id="registry-lifecycle-title">Keep linked records up to date as circumstances change.</h2>
        <p>A population registry office could use Base Registry Engine for a register of residents and households. Staff register people and find existing records. They update details when circumstances change.</p>
        <p>Base Registry Engine stores the information and its history. It does not provide the screens. The office’s own application provides them.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="record-example" aria-labelledby="record-example-caption">
        <div class="stored-records">
          <p class="record-owner">Stored in Base Registry Engine</p>
          <PersonHouseholdRecords />
          <p class="record-storage">Each of the two linked records has its own fields and its own change history.</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="record-lifecycle">
          <li><h3>Register a person</h3><p>Staff create a person’s record and link it to a household. Base Registry Engine checks the required information, the permitted values and the references before it saves the record.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Look up records</h3><p>Staff look up a resident or the people linked to a household. Each application can reach only the records and fields its permissions allow.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Update a record</h3><p>Staff correct a detail or change a household relationship. Base Registry Engine stores the current information and keeps earlier revisions. Authorised users can follow what changed.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <figcaption id="record-example-caption">The person and household records are fictional. The same approach can link a business to its establishments, a professional to their licences or an asset to its location.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="breg-records-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Practical capabilities</p>
        <h2 id="breg-records-title">Define your records and rules once, and every connected service follows them.</h2>
        <p>You define the information, the relationships and the permissions once. Then each of these works with your register under those rules:</p>
        <ul class="prose-list">
          <li>staff applications</li>
          <li>online services</li>
          <li>connected systems</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Define each kind of record</h3><p>You set the kinds of record your register holds. For each kind you also set the required information, the valid values and the relationships.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Control access and approvals</h3><p>You give each staff role or connected service access to the records and fields it needs. Where staff must approve a change, you can connect a review service. One example is our review product, <a href="/products/casework/">Registry Casework</a>. Your register applies approved changes under your own rules.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Keep earlier revisions</h3><p>Your register keeps earlier revisions so authorised staff can see what changed. It stops an older edit from overwriting a newer one. Changes to your register’s structure or rules use reviewed configuration. Where needed, they also use a planned data migration.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="fit" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="breg-fit-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it fits your institution</p>
        <h2 id="breg-fit-title">Build a register that works with the services you already have.</h2>
        <p>Base Registry Engine maintains records behind your staff applications and online services. You can use it to build a new register. You can also use it to move existing records into a defined structure.</p>
        <p>Base Registry Engine does not provide screens. An implementation combines it with the screens, the identity (sign-in) service and the system connections that your service needs.</p>
        <p><a href="/data-spaces/">See how to use a register as a source in a data space</a></p>
        <p><a href="/ai/citizen-access/">See how people can prepare a correction with an approved assistant</a></p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>You define the register</dt><dd>You agree what each record means and how records relate. You also agree which changes are allowed and who may read or change the information.</dd>
        <dt>You choose who delivers it</dt><dd>Your own team or another delivery partner can deliver the implementation. Or we can deliver it for you. The work covers configuring Base Registry Engine and providing the screens. It also covers connecting sign-in and other systems, and planning the data migration. You agree hosting, operation, support and handover in a written scope.</dd>
        <dt>You choose who runs it</dt><dd>Your own team or a provider you appoint can run Base Registry Engine and its database. Or we can host and run them for you. You agree the infrastructure, backups and monitoring. You also agree access management and change procedures. We quote our hosting and operation separately.</dd>
        <dt>You can add checks and reviews</dt><dd>Add <a href="/products/evidence-gateway/">Evidence Gateway</a> when another service needs to check specific information. Add <a href="/products/casework/">Registry Casework</a> when staff must review a request and record a decision.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="developers" class="section" aria-labelledby="breg-developers-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">For developers and implementers</p>
        <h2 id="breg-developers-title">Build and inspect a small register.</h2>
        <p>The introductory tutorial creates a local contact register. It then:</p>
        <ul class="prose-list">
          <li>adds one contact</li>
          <li>reads that contact</li>
          <li>checks that another caller cannot list that contact</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For a real implementation, read the <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/explanation/configuration-defined-registry/">configuration explanation</a> and the <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/operate/breg/">operations documentation</a>.</p>
        <p><a class="inline-cta" href={product.tutorialHref}>{product.tutorialLabel} →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Product>

<style>
  .record-example { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 24px 40px; align-items: start; margin: 0; }
  .stored-records { min-width: 0; background: var(--registry-slate-tint); border: 1px solid var(--registry-slate-rule); padding: clamp(16px, 2vw, 24px); }
  .record-owner { color: var(--registry-blue); font-size: var(--type-sm); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .record-storage { color: var(--registry-body); font-size: var(--type-xs); line-height: 1.6; margin: 16px 0 0; }
  .record-lifecycle { display: grid; gap: 22px; margin: 0; padding-left: 26px; }
  .record-lifecycle li { padding-left: 10px; }
  .record-lifecycle li::marker { color: var(--registry-blue); font-family: var(--registry-mono); font-weight: 600; }
  .record-lifecycle h3 { font-size: var(--type-subhead); margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .record-lifecycle p { margin: 0; }
  .record-example figcaption { grid-column: 1 / -1; color: var(--registry-muted); font-size: var(--type-base); line-height: 1.5; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .record-example { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
